        <note term="a slave of Jesus Christ" kind="departure" lemma="δοῦλος" traditional="servant">The Greek word is the ordinary term for a slave, a person owned by a master, and a first-century reader would have heard it that way. Most English versions soften it to &#x27;servant&#x27;, partly because &#x27;slave of God&#x27; was also an honorific in the Septuagint for figures such as Moses and David. Both the ownership and the honour are probably in view here.</note>
        <note term="kept for Jesus Christ" kind="ambiguity" lemma="τηρέω" traditional="kept for Jesus Christ; preserved in Jesus Christ">The Greek has a bare dative, which can mean &#x27;kept for Jesus Christ&#x27; (safe until his coming) or &#x27;kept by Jesus Christ&#x27;. Most recent translations choose &#x27;for&#x27;. The verb &#x27;keep&#x27; recurs through the letter (verses 6, 13, 21) and is rendered the same way each time.</note>
